WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:01.740
- My art is very much so focused

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:04.134
on my own struggles with mental illness,

00:00:04.134 --> 00:00:05.374
both as a coping mechanism

00:00:05.374 --> 00:00:07.895
but also as a way to kind of articulate

00:00:07.895 --> 00:00:12.805
very precisely how I feel
in a way that's not verbal.

00:00:12.805 --> 00:00:15.799
(gentle music)

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:17.286
My name is Panteha Abareshi.

00:00:17.286 --> 00:00:19.659
I have sickle cell zero beta thalassemia.

00:00:19.659 --> 00:00:22.318
It's a genetic blood condition.

00:00:22.318 --> 00:00:23.401
My mom had an

00:00:24.412 --> 00:00:27.061
unexpressed gene for sickle cell.

00:00:27.061 --> 00:00:29.701
And my dad had an unexpressed
gene for thalassemia.

00:00:29.701 --> 00:00:34.331
And I got the least likely but
worst combination of genes,

00:00:34.331 --> 00:00:35.966
where I have both of them expressed.

00:00:35.966 --> 00:00:37.353
So, I have both of the diseases.

00:00:37.353 --> 00:00:40.343
The pain aspect of it
comes from the blood cells

00:00:40.343 --> 00:00:43.960
getting caught between
my bones or sickling.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.070
And creating this pain response

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:50.007
that can manifest in
bones, joints, nerves.

00:00:50.007 --> 00:00:52.308
When I turned 14, the
pain, all of a sudden,

00:00:52.308 --> 00:00:55.380
became much more severe and constant.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.476
So, it was a big change
to all of a sudden,

00:00:59.476 --> 00:01:01.342
like not having the pain ever subside

00:01:01.342 --> 00:01:02.956
and to kind of learn
how to live with that.

00:01:02.956 --> 00:01:05.887
I was like isolated and in
the hospital all the time,

00:01:05.887 --> 00:01:07.450
at home all the time.

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:10.407
When my pain started getting really bad

00:01:10.407 --> 00:01:13.006
and my depression and anxiety
started getting really bad,

00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:15.210
I kind of lost all of the things

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:16.892
that I liked to do or the things

00:01:16.892 --> 00:01:19.166
that I would turn to for coping.

00:01:19.166 --> 00:01:20.695
Whether it was like reading books

00:01:20.695 --> 00:01:22.686
or even listening to music or watching TV.

00:01:22.686 --> 00:01:25.042
It just kind of would like be just noise

00:01:25.042 --> 00:01:28.069
that I couldn't really focus on or enjoy.

00:01:28.069 --> 00:01:30.486
I never just like picked up
a pen and a piece of paper

00:01:30.486 --> 00:01:32.835
and been like, "I'm gonna just draw."

00:01:32.835 --> 00:01:35.411
So, (laughs) when I started doing that,

00:01:35.411 --> 00:01:38.941
it was like the most amazing feeling.

00:01:38.941 --> 00:01:41.670
I need something, you know,
obviously like everyone does,

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.286
when you're in pain to distract you.

00:01:43.286 --> 00:01:46.674
But my art, I really quickly realized

00:01:46.674 --> 00:01:49.319
was what I needed to cope
with my mental illness

00:01:49.319 --> 00:01:51.096
that was very, very intense.

00:01:51.096 --> 00:01:55.010
My sickle cell, like,
people actually understand

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:56.284
and are actively trying to help me.

00:01:56.284 --> 00:01:57.117
And with my mental illness,

00:01:57.117 --> 00:01:59.068
people are like, "Well,
I don't see the symptoms,

00:01:59.068 --> 00:02:01.636
"then I don't care and you're better."

00:02:01.636 --> 00:02:03.310
Honestly, it all comes
back down to the fact

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.344
that it's so stigmatized
and misunderstood.

00:02:05.344 --> 00:02:07.927
(gentle music)

00:02:11.302 --> 00:02:13.552
Creating my art for myself,

00:02:14.883 --> 00:02:17.423
and not for anyone else is
like consumption, really.

00:02:17.423 --> 00:02:18.716
Like, I'm not putting it out there

00:02:18.716 --> 00:02:21.819
expecting people to like
it or to want to see more.

00:02:21.819 --> 00:02:24.025
And all the girls that I
draw, like they love roses.

00:02:24.025 --> 00:02:26.391
Roses is kind of like this weapon almost.

00:02:26.391 --> 00:02:27.925
Like, the things that are like spiney.

00:02:27.925 --> 00:02:29.234
Like, you could like cut someone with it

00:02:29.234 --> 00:02:31.169
or you know, impale someone with it.

00:02:31.169 --> 00:02:35.284
When I draw my girls, or
the people that I draw,

00:02:35.284 --> 00:02:38.738
and they are in pain, the literal sense,

00:02:38.738 --> 00:02:40.286
like the physical sense of pain.

00:02:40.286 --> 00:02:43.351
Where there's like a knife in their neck

00:02:43.351 --> 00:02:45.889
or like a rose poking through their hand.

00:02:45.889 --> 00:02:47.437
Or like they're squeezing a rose so hard

00:02:47.437 --> 00:02:48.855
that blood's dripping down their hands.

00:02:48.855 --> 00:02:50.529
Or like, they have a bloody nose.

00:02:50.529 --> 00:02:52.209
Or like, they're bleeding
from their mouth.

00:02:52.209 --> 00:02:54.880
Like, things that you associate with,

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:55.864
"Whoa, something's wrong."

00:02:55.864 --> 00:02:57.119
Like, "That must've really hurt."

00:02:57.119 --> 00:03:00.629
Like, all of these things
that are painful, dangerous.

00:03:00.629 --> 00:03:01.841
Like, you look at them and you're like,

00:03:01.841 --> 00:03:03.646
"Ow, that looks like it really hurts."

00:03:03.646 --> 00:03:04.646
Those are me

00:03:06.114 --> 00:03:09.262
expressing what my
mental illness feels like

00:03:09.262 --> 00:03:12.116
in ways that people can like take in

00:03:12.116 --> 00:03:14.283
and understand a degree of pain.

00:03:14.283 --> 00:03:16.525
Creating something
tangible out of something

00:03:16.525 --> 00:03:18.459
that is like hyper intangible.

00:03:18.459 --> 00:03:22.904
To share a pain or to like
share a sensation like that,

00:03:22.904 --> 00:03:24.358
like a hurt sensation,

00:03:24.358 --> 00:03:25.697
it makes people feel uncomfortable

00:03:25.697 --> 00:03:27.218
and it makes people feel vulnerable.

00:03:27.218 --> 00:03:30.159
And that's what I think
we need people to feel

00:03:30.159 --> 00:03:31.634
in order for them to understand

00:03:31.634 --> 00:03:33.091
honestly what mental illness is like.

00:03:33.091 --> 00:03:35.008
And to like make a
change and move forward.

00:03:35.008 --> 00:03:37.591
(gentle music)

00:03:48.363 --> 00:03:50.946
(sign squeaks)

